# not just an upvalue!
# 
# When the main chunk executes:
# - Register 0 = _ENV (which should point to the globals table)
# - But the upvalue[0] is set to globals table (in f_parser)
# 
# The issue: in our setup, _ENV is at REGISTER 0 (a local variable).
# When we compile "print", it finds _ENV as VLOCAL at register 0.
# Then luaK_indexed creates VINDEXSTR with ind.t = 0 (register 0).
# 
# When executing: GETTABUP 0, 0, K["print"]
# Wait, for VINDEXSTR (not VINDEXUP), the opcode is GETFIELD, not GETTABUP!
# GETFIELD: R[A] = R[B]["key"] where B is the register of the table
# 
# But R[0] at execution time is... what?
# 
# For the main chunk with _ENV as a local variable:
# - The chunk expects register 0 to hold the globals table
# - But we never LOAD the globals table into register 0!
# 
# Looking at the VM: when a new Lua function is called,
# the registers start ABOVE the function slot.
# For the main chunk: ci->func is the function slot, registers start at ci->func+1.
# 
# Since _ENV was added as "local variable 0", the VARARGPREP instruction
# (if present) should adjust the stack.
# 
# But we're using VINDEXSTR which expects the table to be in a REGISTER!
# For global access, we should use GETTABUP (upvalue access), not GETFIELD (register access)!
# 
# The correct approach in Lua 5.4:
# _ENV is set up as upvalue[0], NOT as a local variable.
# When singlevaraux looks for a name not found locally:
# It calls singlevaraux on the parent to find _ENV.
# Since there's no parent (main function), _ENV is set as a KNOWN upvalue.
# 
# The original Lua setup is more subtle. Let me re-read:
# In Lua 5.4's lparser.c, the main function gets _ENV via:
# 1. The lexer sets ls->envn = luaS_newliteral(L, LUA_ENV)
# 2. In luaY_parser, after open_func:
#    - Adds _ENV as a "proto upvalue" (funcstate.nups = 1)
#    - But in luaF_initupvals, this upvalue gets its value from the lapi.c code
# 
# Actually the standard Lua approach:
# For the main chunk, _ENV is upvalue[0] with instack=1, idx=0.
# This means it's at stack position 0 OF THE CALLING FUNCTION.
# But the main chunk has no calling Lua function!
# 
# In lua.c (ldo.c f_parser):
# After loading the closure, upvalue[0]->v.p is set to point to the globals table.
# This is done directly by setting the upvalue value pointer.
# 
# So the correct behavior is:
# - _ENV is an UPVALUE (not a local var) of the main chunk
# - upvalue[0]->v.p points to the globals table
# - When code accesses _ENV, it's via GETTABUP (which uses upvalue, not register)
# 
# THE FIX: Remove the "local variable" setup of _ENV, and instead just
# set up the upvalue directly (without creating a local var).
# 
